{"id":"df209182-844e-45a8-802e-7b82328d0815","object":"chat.completion","created":1793869873,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Now let me confirm the module-level init in logger.py and any other log-emitting spots in stats_logger.\n\n```python\nr=read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\",68,96); print(r['content'])\nprint(\"=== search stats logger.info\")\nprint(search_code(\"logger.info\",\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\",20))\nprint(\"=== environ imports logger?\")\nprint(search_code(\"import logger\",\"deepinfer/environ.py\",10))\n```","reasoning_content":"Now I have everything I need. Let me make the edits.\n\n1. environ.py: add helper `_log_max_message_chars` and descriptor. Need int parse >=32, None if unset, empty string raises ValueError including variable name. Note the descriptor wraps exceptions with the name already (`f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`). But the error text must contain variable name — the wrapper does this. However for TypeError-rewrap case... Actually the wrapper: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") from e` catches TypeError and raises original. For ValueError, we'd get \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: invalid literal...\". Good. But safer: include name in helper message too? The value error from int() is fine since wrapper adds the name. But the helper is also used... let me include a message anyway; double name is harmless. Actually careful: empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\". Wrapped → contains name. Good.\n\nAdd factory method to EnvVar: `positive_int_or_none(min_value)` — hmm, spec says descriptor class can add helper factory methods. Let's add:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_at_least_or_none(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nWait: unset means None. But what about empty string? `int(\"\")` → ValueError. Good.\n\nAdd config item in Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_at_least_or_none(minimum=32)\n    \"\"\"普通诊断日志消息正文的最大字符数；未设置表示不限长。\"\"\"\n```\n\n2. logger.py: formatter. Implementation:\n\n```python\n_MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET = 32\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _validate_max_message_chars(max_message_chars: int | None) -> int | None:\n    if max_message_chars is None:\n        return None\n    if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n        raise ValueError(...)\n    if max_message_chars < _MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n    return max_message_chars\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= budget:\n        return message\n    marker = _OMITTED_MARKER\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else message[:head] + marker\n```\n\nCareful with budget >= 32 so remaining >= 15, head=8, tail=7? Wait spec: budget 32 → head 9, tail 8, marker 15. remaining = 32-15 = 17. ceil(17/2) = 9, tail = 8. Yes (remaining+1)//2 = 9. Good.\n\nFor tail==0 handling, with budget>=32 remaining>=17 so tail>=8 always. But be safe.\n\nNow in format: need message body after args substitution but before adding prefix. `record.getMessage()` performs %s substitution without mutating record. So:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            if truncated != message:\n                record = _copy_record_with_message(record, truncated)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, but requirement 5: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info；同一记录随后交给完整处理器，仍须保留完整消息。\" So make a copy via copy.copy? copy module isn't in allowed imports list... allowed imports: collections, datetime, itertools, json, math, queue, random, re, stat, statistics, time, unicodedata. But that's for my action code, not for the source file. Source file can use any stdlib. Ok.\n\nBut wait—super().format(record) also formats exception info. If we copy record, exc_info preserved. Use `copy.copy(record)` then set msg=truncated, args=(). Let's do:\n\n```python\n        buf = record  # ...\n        if ...:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(...)\n            if truncated != message:\n                buf = copy.copy(record)\n                buf.msg = truncated\n                buf.args = ()\n```\n\nBut `record.message` is set by super().format; the copy will compute message again = truncated. And the multiline prefix code uses `record.message` — use buf.message instead. Actually the existing code:\n\n```python\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n```\nafter super().format(buf), buf.message == truncated. So use buf.\n\nAlso important: not mutating original record's `msg`/`args` — copy.copy gives shallow copy; record.exc_info shared reference, fine (we don't modify).\n\nNeed `import copy` in logger.py.\n\nAlso note: the record may already have `message` attribute set by a previous handler's formatting; super().format on the original record would set record.message. If our handler copies before calling super().format on original, record.message not set yet. Fine.\n\nEdge: if truncated message equals message... only if message <= budget, then unchanged.\n\nAlso color and multiline handling remain.\n\nAlso